The cited theorem is specific: Kim–Roush Theorem 7 proves NP-completeness when the public polynomial has degree \(2n\), the two factors have equal degree \(n\), endpoint coefficients are zero, and public coefficients lie in \(\{0,1,2,3\}\). It also identifies easy cases: convex coefficient diagrams factor into linear terms in polynomial time (Proposition 3), fixed degree reduces to finitely many linear programs, and low-degree-divisor brute force is effective unless endpoint zeros are enforced. I’m using the paper’s equal-degree, endpoint-zero regime with degree growing, and I’ll state honestly that worst-case NP-completeness does not prove average-case hardness for the random planted distribution.
